mps":{"offense":[{"season":2023,"team":"GB","distance":0.411,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Matt LaFleur"},{"season":2025,"team":"LV","distance":0.4852,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Chip Kelly"},{"season":2023,"team":"LAC","distance":0.5441,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"},{"season":2023,"team":"TB","distance":0.5509,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2022,"team":"GB","distance":0.5645,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Matt LaFleur"}],"defense":[{"season":2020,"team":"CIN","distance":0.6383,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2023,"team":"CIN","distance":0.6412,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2022,"team":"CHI","distance":0.662,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"NE","distance":0.6814,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"DET","distance":0.6858,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Eagles went 4-11-1 in 2020 and were outscored 418 to 334. The defense did most of the work, finishing 13th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 30th in EPA per play. What set them apart was the 2nd highest 12 personnel usage and the 2nd lowest 2-Man rate in the league.","offense":"The Eagles offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd highest 12 personnel usage and the lowest I formation usage in the league. Carson Wentz ran the passing game, with Greg Ward leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Eagles defense was a little above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was sack rate, where it ranked 3rd, and its weakest was takeaways per game, where it ranked 22nd. Brandon Graham was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Josh Sweat, who had 6 sacks despite playing only 43% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd lowest 2-Man rate in the league.","special":"The Eagles were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 50% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 74% of field goals, which was one of the worst in the league. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their linebackers were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Carson Wentz played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,620 yards. Targets were spread around, with Greg Ward and Zach Ertz leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Miles Sanders handled most of the work in the backfield with 60% of the carries. On defense, Brandon Graham led the team with 8 sacks and Marcus Epps picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Packers under Matt LaFleur and the 2025 Raiders under Chip Kelly. Their defense looked most like the 2020 and 2023 Bengals, both under Lou Anarumo.","trends":"The offense went from 18th in EPA per play in 2019 to 30th in 2020, a big step back. The defense moved from 13th in EPA allowed per play in 2019 to 13th in 2020, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was pistol usage, which went from 0% to 2%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2020. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}